"[2511.09554] RF-DETR: Neural Architecture Search for Real-Time Detection Transformers (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09554)\nciteturn5977view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09554\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 3 Feb 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:RF-DETR: Neural Architecture Search for Real-Time Detection Transformers\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Isaac Robinson , cite7†Peter Robicheaux , cite8†Matvei Popov , cite9†Deva Ramanan , cite10†Neehar Peri L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled RF-DETR: Neural Architecture Search for Real-Time Detection Transformers, by Isaac Robinson and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Open-vocabulary detectors achieve impressive performance on COCO, but often fail to generalize to real-world datasets with out-of-distribution classes not typically found in their pre-training. Rather than simply fine-tuning a heavy-weight vision-language model (VLM) for new domains, we introduce RF-DETR, a light-weight specialist detection transformer that discovers accuracy-latency Pareto curves for any target dataset with weight-sharing neural architecture search (NAS).\nL17: Our approach fine-tunes a pre-trained base network on a target dataset and evaluates thousands of network configurations with different accuracy-latency tradeoffs without re-training. Further, we revisit the \"tunable knobs\" for NAS to improve the transferability of DETRs to diverse target domains. Notably, RF-DETR significantly improves over prior state-of-the-art real-time methods on COCO and Roboflow100-VL.\nL18: RF-DETR (nano) achieves 48.0 AP on COCO, beating D-FINE (nano) by 5.3 AP at similar latency, and RF-DETR (2x-large) outperforms GroundingDINO (tiny) by 1.2 AP on Roboflow100-VL while running 20x as fast. To the best of our knowledge, RF-DETR (2x-large) is the first real-time detector to surpass 60 AP on COCO. Our code is available at cite13†this https URL†github.com L19: Comments:  | This work has been accepted to the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) 2026. Project Page: cite14†this https URL†rfdetr.roboflow.com L20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2511.09554 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2511.09554v2 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09554†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Neehar Peri [cite18†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 18:58:39 UTC (11,009 KB)\nL28: [v2] Tue, 3 Feb 2026 23:53:20 UTC (11,605 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled RF-DETR: Neural Architecture Search for Real-Time Detection Transformers, by Isaac Robinson and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite19†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite20†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite21†< prev |   cite22†next > L46: \nL47: cite23†new | cite24†recent | cite25†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite26†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09139] MACEval: A Multi-Agent Continual Evaluation Network for Large Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09139)\nciteturn5977view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09139\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 30 Jan 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:MACEval: A Multi-Agent Continual Evaluation Network for Large Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Zijian Chen , cite7†Yuze Sun , cite8†Yuan Tian , cite9†Wenjun Zhang , cite10†Guangtao Zhai L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled MACEval: A Multi-Agent Continual Evaluation Network for Large Models, by Zijian Chen and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Hundreds of benchmarks dedicated to evaluating large models have been presented over the past few years. However, most of them remain closed-ended and are prone to overfitting due to the potential data contamination. Moreover, the increasing scale and scope of current benchmarks with transient metrics, as well as the heavily human-dependent curation procedure, pose significant challenges for timely maintenance and adaptation.\nL17: In this paper, we introduce MACEval, a Multi-Agent Continual Evaluation network for dynamic evaluation of large models, and define new metrics to quantify performance longitudinally. MACEval employs an interactive and autonomous evaluation mode, utilizing role assignment, in-process data generation, and evaluation routing through a cascaded agent network.\nL18: Extensive experiments on 23 large models demonstrate the effectiveness of MACEval, which also lightens the evaluation process and reduces a considerable amount of overhead. We hope that MACEval can broaden future directions of large model evaluation. Project page: cite13†this https URL†github.com .\nL19: Comments:  | 32 pages, 14 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.09139 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.09139v2 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09139†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Zijian Chen [cite17†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 09:26:24 UTC (5,092 KB)\nL28: [v2] Fri, 30 Jan 2026 05:33:23 UTC (8,069 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled MACEval: A Multi-Agent Continual Evaluation Network for Large Models, by Zijian Chen and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite31†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite32†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.08917] \"It's trained by non-disabled people\": Evaluating How Image Quality Affects Product Captioning with Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08917)\nciteturn5977view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08917\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Human-Computer Interaction\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 31 Mar 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:\"It's trained by non-disabled people\": Evaluating How Image Quality Affects Product Captioning with Vision-Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Kapil Garg , cite7†Xinru Tang , cite8†Jimin Heo , cite9†Dwayne R. Morgan , cite10†Darren Gergle , cite11†Erik B. Sudderth , cite12†Anne Marie Piper L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled \"It's trained by non-disabled people\": Evaluating How Image Quality Affects Product Captioning with Vision-Language Models, by Kapil Garg and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are increasingly used by blind and low-vision (BLV) people to identify and understand products in their everyday lives, such as food, personal care items, and household goods. Despite their prevalence, we lack an empirical understanding of how common image quality issues--such as blur, misframing, and rotation--affect the accuracy of VLM-generated captions and whether the resulting captions meet BLV people's information needs.\nL17: Based on a survey of 86 BLV participants, we develop an annotated dataset of 1,859 product images from BLV people to systematically evaluate how image quality issues affect VLM-generated captions. While the best VLM achieves 98% accuracy on images with no quality issues, accuracy drops to 75% overall when quality issues are present, worsening considerably as issues compound.\nL18: We discuss the need for model evaluations that center on disabled people's experiences throughout the process and offer concrete recommendations for HCI and ML researchers to make VLMs more reliable for BLV people.\nL19: Comments:  | Published at CHI 2026; Honorable Mention for Best Paper (Top 5%). Dataset available at: cite15†this https URL†github.com L20: Subjects:  | Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.08917 [cs.HC]\nL22:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.08917v3 [cs.HC] for this version)\nL23:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08917†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite19†https://doi.org/10.1145/3772318.3791309†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Kapil Garg [cite20†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 02:54:13 UTC (29,971 KB)\nL29: cite21†[v2] Sat, 22 Nov 2025 22:58:28 UTC (11,977 KB)\nL30